# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets — NEVER commit. See .env.example for the variable list.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python / uv
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
.eggs/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tool caches — machine-specific, regenerated on every run
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.workbuddy-ai/
.review-evidence/
.review-probe/
# `tests/` is TRACKED (re-tracked 2026-10-08): the test suite is the evidence
# for `src/`, and CI runs it. Only the operator tooling below stays local.
scripts/
tools/
# Pinned-basetemp directories. `pytest --basetemp=.pytest_<name>` is how this
# project keeps a run's temp tree INSIDE the repo (D-087.26: a `--basetemp`
# outside the project is what a sandbox bulk-delete counter refuses to clean,
# leaving a zero-length summary and a spurious exit 1). Any such tree holds
# sandbox COPIES of real source — including copies of `scripts/mutation_*.py` —
# so committing one puts a mutant-shaped file in the repo. Measured on
# 2026-09-22: three of these (`.pytest_dur2`, `.pytest_iso`, `.pytest_g1`) were
# picked up by the operator's own `git add .` run from the repo root (commits
# `43019ea` added them, `5951dae` removed them; neither ever reached origin/main
# in a tree that still held them), and `ruff format --check .` then reported a
# 251st file that is not source at all. `git add .` cannot re-add these now.
.pytest_tmp/
.pytest_*/
!.pytest_cache/
# Other pinned-basetemp spellings, same reasoning as the block above. A gate run
# uses `--basetemp=.gate_pt` (D-088) and that tree holds sandbox COPIES of real
# source; committing one would put a copy of a `scripts/mutation_*.py` in the
# repo and add a non-source file to the `ruff format --check` count. Added so
# the spelling cannot produce the 251-file defect the `.pytest_*` rules were
# written to prevent.
.gate_pt/
.gate_pt*/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
coverage.xml


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Probe scratch tree — untracked by design (see O-44 / O-63).
# These scripts are exploratory and are NOT part of the project's gate scope
# (`src tests tools scripts`). Keeping them out of the repo is what makes a
# bare `ruff check` misfire visible rather than invisible.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.probe/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Raw data store — 131 parquet files pulled from OpenBB/FRED.
# Regenerable from the series registry; pinning market data in git would make
# every snapshot a multi-MB blob and would let a stale vintage masquerade as
# current. The registry (config/series_registry.yaml) IS committed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
data/raw/
data/cache/
*.parquet

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WorkBuddy local state — agent memory is useful but is not source.
# Uncomment the second line if you would rather keep project notes local.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.workbuddy-ai/memory/*.md
# .workbuddy-ai/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OS / editor
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*~

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mutation-sweep recovery sidecars (D-082)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `scripts/_sweep_gate.record_pristine()` writes a target's pristine text to
# `<name>.sweepbackup` before mutating it, so a sweep killed on a platform with
# no usable signal handler (win32) can still be restored on the next run. The
# sidecar is deleted on a clean exit; one left behind means a run was killed,
# and it must NEVER be committed as a source file.
*.sweepbackup
